  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 4 December 2024 Australia&#x2019;s Minister for Foreign Affairs launched the Southeast Asia Maritime Institute, hosted by the Australian National Centre for Ocean Resources and Security (ANCORS) in Malaysia. The Institute is bolstering law of the sea and maritime security education and expertise for the region. The Institute is delivering training to officials from across Southeast Asia on maritime legal and policy issues, including ocean governance, maritime domain awareness, maritime environmental protection, and law of the sea. This aims to improve collective maritime capabilities and promote the rules and norms that support a peaceful, stable and prosperous region. Since opening, the institute has hosted recipients of scholarships for the Graduate Certificate in Maritime Security; a Women in Maritime Security short course on law of the sea; and other lectures, workshops and meetings for maritime officials and experts from across Southeast Asia.  Australia also provided other postgraduate scholarships through ANCORS to further increase expertise for current and emerging maritime security leaders in Southeast Asia.   ANCORS Scholarships for Master&#x2019;s of Maritime Policy (7) and Master&#x2019;s of Maritime Laws (1) were provided to officials from the governments of Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, and Vietnam.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The 2024 Southeast Asian Women in Maritime Security Network (WIMS), part of Australia&#x2019;s commitment to support the Women, Peace, and Security agenda, invited women working in defence, civil maritime security and foreign affairs to undertake a two-week program in Australia focusing on maritime security and women&#x2019;s leadership. Eighteen participants from Brunei, Malaysia, the Philippines, Vietnam, Indonesia and Australia participated in interactive lectures and field trips to deepen understanding of the law of the sea and build a network to enhance women&#x2019;s leadership and inclusion in the maritime security sector. Australia also launched the Southeast Asian Maritime Security Scholarships, inviting eight emerging maritime security leaders from Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, and Vietnam to undertake Masters degrees and PhDs in maritime security. The scholarships will strengthen the expertise of maritime security leaders. Both initiatives were delivered by the Australian National Centre for Ocean Resources and Security at the University of Wollongong.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The third annual Southeast Asia Women in Maritime Security (WIMS) Program was held over two weeks in May 2025, coinciding with the International Day of Women in Maritime. Delivered by the Australian National Centre for Ocean Resources and Security, the objective of the program is to enhance knowledge and skills in sea law, security and leadership for women working in the male-dominated maritime sector. In 2025, WIMS brought together 17 female officials working in foreign affairs, defence and civil maritime security roles from Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Vietnam, Australia, and for the first time Japan. The course provided classroom activities (lectures and tabletop exercises) and field trips to Australian maritime security facilities (including naval bases and coast guard headquarters). The integration of the law of the sea and leadership modules created valuable interdisciplinary connections and was valued by participants. In the evaluation survey, a participant said  &#x201c;the combination of strategic learning and real-world exposure... gave us firsthand insight into operational practices and how gender perspectives are integrated&#x201d;; while another reflected &#x201c;I will bring home best practices and align them with the Philippine Navy&#x2019;s vision of becoming a modern, multi-capable force by 2028&#x201d;.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
